st on Main Street to identify the steep saltbox roof and split-wood red-oak shingles described on the heritage trail.","short_summary":"A named pre-Revolution-style house on the West Main Street corridor.","evidence_claim":"The heritage trail lists the Log House at 437 W. Main St. as a pre-Revolution New England saltbox-style house with a steep roof and split-wood red-oak shingles.","source_url":"https://visitlebanonvalley.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/Annville_HeritageTrail_Map.pdf","source_title":"Annville Heritage Trail Map","source_kind":"cultural_institution","source_published_at":null,"source_fetched_at":"2026-08-13","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"maps_url":null,"access_notes":"Exterior viewing only; this is a residential property.","cost_status":"unknown","under21_safe":true},{"subject_id":"flour-mill","subject_type":"street_or_district","entity_name":"Flour Mill","title":"Flour Mill: Annville’s long-running milling site","subtitle":"West Queen Street corridor — 545 W. Queen St.","notes":"Detour one block south of West Main Street to see the four-story frame-and-stone mill complex noted by the heritage trail.","short_summary":"A historic mill complex on the West Queen Street corridor.","evidence_claim":"The heritage trail identifies the Flour Mill at 545 W. Queen St. and states that a precursor is attested as early as March 10, 1752.","source_url":"https://visitlebanonvalley.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/Annville_HeritageTrail_Map.pdf","source_title":"Annville Heritage Trail Map","source_kind":"cultural_institution","source_published_at":null,"source_fetched_at":"2026-08-13","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"maps_url":null,"access_notes":"View the complex from public areas; confirm any on-site access with its current operator.","cost_status":"unknown","under21_safe":true},{"subject_id":"jerusalem-cemetery","subject_type":"street_or_district","entity_name":"Jerusalem Cemetery","title":"Jerusalem Cemetery: an early Annville burial ground","subtitle":"South White Oak Street corridor — 200 S. White Oak St.","notes":"Take South White Oak Street to this named cemetery site and read the trail’s account of Annville pioneers buried there in the 1800s.","short_summary":"A documented early burial-ground site on South White Oak Street.","evidence_claim":"The heritage trail lists Jerusalem Cemetery at 200 S. White Oak St. and says many Annville pioneers were buried there between 1804 and 1895.","source_url":"https://visitlebanonvalley.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/Annville_HeritageTrail_Map.pdf","source_title":"Annville Heritage Trail Map","source_kind":"cultural_institution","source_published_at":null,"source_fetched_at":"2026-08-13","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"maps_url":null,"access_notes":"Treat the site as a cemetery and follow posted rules and any access restrictions.","cost_status":"unknown","under21_safe":true}],"warnings":[]}],"warnings":[]}
